## Langfuse explains the boundaries behind Halo’s background investigations

Source: Langfuse — https://langfuse.com/blog/2026-10-21-building-the-langfuse-assistant
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: news

Langfuse published an engineering account of Halo, its existing in-app assistant, rather than announcing a new launch. The design reuses the public MCP server and shared instructions so improvements also reach external agents. Each run receives a temporary project-scoped key; available tools are filtered by the signed-in user’s role, and changes require approval.

The agent loop stays in backend workers. An optional sandbox handles files and scripts without network access by default, receiving data only after authorized MCP calls. Large tool results can be saved to files instead of filling the model’s context. That separates data access from computation while allowing code to summarize many observations.

Postgres stores runs and events, while BullMQ delivers work independently of the browser. Failed runs are not automatically retried, a limitation the authors explicitly contrast with durable workflow engines. Langfuse also reports using its own traces to identify broken prompt caching and improve tool errors. The account offers implementation detail, not a quantified reliability or security guarantee.

Why it matters: Review access boundaries and failure recovery separately when adding investigative agents to observability products.

