## Phoenix evaluators adopt decision models with explicit refusal behavior

Source: Phoenix — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/%40arizeai/phoenix-evals%402.7.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

Phoenix Evals 2.7.0 lets classification evaluators, including its built-in evaluators, run on AI SDK decision models. The October 21 release says labels now come from experimental_decide, with label probabilities and provider confidence, when available, recorded in metadata. Decision spans capture the decision state, question and answer. The companion Phoenix OTEL 2.3.0 release upgrades its OpenInference Vercel tracing dependency for decision-model support.

This is also a behavioral migration for existing evaluation models such as TypeSafe’s Jev. They use the same decision API, and message prompts are joined into plain text rather than serialized as JSON state. Refusals throw the SDK’s Experimental_DecisionRefusalError instead of becoming an ordinary classification result. Those distinctions matter when downstream code aggregates labels or treats missing results as successes.

The release pins ai to 7.0.137 and @ai-sdk/otel to 1.0.137 because the experimental interface has changed in patch versions. It specifies @ai-sdk/openai 4.0.89 or later for OpenAI decision models. These are compatibility notes, not evidence that classifier accuracy or probability calibration has improved.

Why it matters: Regression-test prompt rendering, refusal handling and recorded metadata before comparing new classifier runs with historical scores.

