## Braintrust adds application logs alongside broader agent tracing

Source: Braintrust — https://github.com/braintrustdata/braintrust-sdk-javascript/releases/tag/braintrust%403.39.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

Braintrust’s JavaScript SDK 3.39.0 expands what can be recorded around an agent execution. The October 21 release adds application logging and console capture alongside new AI SDK batch tracing and code-mode tracing. It also adds LiveKit cascade-mode instrumentation and extends Cloudflare Think support across versions 0.13 through 0.20. These are distinct integration changes, rather than a claim that every application path is automatically observable.

The patch notes also address fidelity within existing integrations. Streamed Bedrock InvokeModel calls to OpenAI models now log text and usage. Pi coding-agent and Flue integrations include cache reads and writes in prompt_tokens. For OpenAI, OpenRouter and Hugging Face, streamed tool calls stay separate when a provider omits the index field. Incorrectly joining calls or omitting usage can make a trace look coherent while misrepresenting what actually happened.

For operators, application logs can provide context that is absent from model spans, but capture coverage and the content retained still need inspection in a real deployment. The release notes do not supply an independent overhead benchmark or certify cost-accounting accuracy. Teams comparing token totals across versions should account for the changed cache-token handling.

Why it matters: Test representative batch, code, voice and streamed-tool paths, and reconcile token totals before using upgraded traces for cost comparisons.

