## Phoenix adds frontend-error reporting and tightens runtime dependencies

Source: Phoenix — https://github.com/Arize-ai/phoenix/releases/tag/arize-phoenix-v20.24.0
Published: 2026-10-21
Channel: github

Phoenix 20.24.0 adds reporting of uncaught frontend errors to the server log. For teams operating an evaluation and tracing interface, a failure in the browser is a different failure surface from an application span or a server exception. Bringing that error into server-side logs gives operators another place to look when users encounter a broken screen, although the release does not describe alerting behavior or report end-to-end detection tests.

The same release tightens several dependency constraints. Its notes exclude LiteLLM 1.107.0, raise the certifi minimum to 2026.10.18 and require patched Node releases. These are specific package and runtime requirements, not a deployment-wide assurance. Checking the full release notes alongside resolved versions is more useful than assuming that a package upgrade also replaced every runtime or transitive dependency.

Operators should therefore distinguish updating the Phoenix package from verifying the resolved software actually running in their environment. A lockfile, container image and runtime can each preserve older components. That verification is an operational follow-up, not a guarantee supplied by the version number or by the new frontend logging feature.

Why it matters: Check resolved dependencies and exercise browser-error reporting in the deployed environment rather than treating an upgrade label as sufficient verification.

